IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `scta-jurisdiction`

-- Small Claims Tribunals Act 1984 (Singapore) -- what the tribunal can order,
-- and what can be done about it afterwards: ss 34, 35, 36, 38, 40, 41, 42
-- and 46.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules
-- skill. No pipeline, no independent test pass, no human gate.

§ `Small Claims Tribunals Act 1984 -- orders and appeal`

§§ `35(1) -- the eight orders`

-- s 35(1): a tribunal "MAY, as regards any claim WITHIN ITS JURISDICTION, make
-- one or more of the following orders, subject to such conditions ... as the
-- tribunal thinks fit to impose:
--   (a) an order requiring a party to pay money to another party;
--   (b) a WORK ORDER against a party;
--   (c) an order requiring a party to do anything under a work order within a
--       specified time and, in default, to pay money to a specified person;
--   (d) an order dismissing the whole or part of the claim;
--   (e) an order to vary a work order;
--   (f) an order requiring a party to pay COSTS to another party;
--   (g) where the claim is for UNPAID RENT for any premises under a contract
--       specified in paragraph 1(c) of the Schedule, an order for the DELIVERY OF
--       VACANT POSSESSION of the premises;
--   (h) any ancillary order necessary to give effect to any order made."
--
-- s 2(1): "'work order' means an order to rectify a defect in goods or any damage
-- to property, or to make good any deficiency in the performance of services, by
-- doing such work or attending to such matters (including the replacement of
-- goods or parts thereof) as may be specified in the order."
--
-- The work order is the thing this tribunal has that a money court does not: it
-- can require the builder to come back and fix the floor rather than pay for
-- someone else to. Limb (c) backs it with a money order in default, which is how
-- it is made to bite.
--
-- Limb (g) is the one that will surprise a reader. On a claim for unpaid rent
-- under a residential lease of not more than two years, the Small Claims
-- Tribunal can order the tenant out. The jurisdiction is confined to Schedule
-- para 1(c) and to a claim for unpaid rent -- not to any other breach of the
-- lease -- but within that it is a possession order from a tribunal designed for
-- consumer disputes.
DECLARE OrderSought IS ONE OF
    `money`
    `a work order`
    `a work order with money in default`
    `dismissal of the claim`
    `variation of a work order`
    `costs`
    `delivery of vacant possession`
    `an ancillary order`

GIVEN `the order` IS AN OrderSought, `the claim` IS A Claim, `the claim is for unpaid rent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this order is available on this claim` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `delivery of vacant possession`
        THEN     `the claim is within Schedule paragraph 1(c)` (`the claim`'s `the subject`)
             AND `the claim is for unpaid rent`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `35(2) -- what the cap applies to, and what it does not`

-- s 35(2): "The total value of a MONEY ORDER OR WORK ORDER made under
-- subsection (1) must not exceed -- (a) in the case where section 5(4) applies,
-- the prescribed extended limit; and (b) in any other case, the prescribed
-- limit."
-- s 35(4): "'money order' means an order under subsection (1)(a)."
--
-- So the cap reaches limb (a) and limb (b), and on its face reaches neither the
-- costs order under limb (f) nor the possession order under limb (g) nor the
-- ancillary order under limb (h). A possession order has no "total value" in the
-- subsection's sense, and nothing in the Act values the premises or caps the
-- order by reference to them. The tenant of a property worth any amount may be
-- ordered out on a rent claim of $500.
--
-- The cap is also the only place the extended limit does any work after
-- jurisdiction is settled: where the parties signed a memorandum under s 5(4),
-- the order may go to $30,000.
GIVEN `the order` IS AN OrderSought
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 35(2) caps this kind of order` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `money` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a work order` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a work order with money in default` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the ceiling on a money or work order` IS
    IF   `section 5(4) applies to the claim` `the claim` `the limits`
    THEN `the limits`'s `the prescribed extended limit`
    ELSE `the limits`'s `the prescribed limit`

-- A claimant who abandoned the excess under s 9 is not within s 5(4), so the
-- ceiling on the order stays at the prescribed limit. That is the arithmetic of
-- the abandonment: the claim is heard, and the order cannot exceed $20,000.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A Limits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `abandoning the excess leaves the ceiling at the prescribed limit` IF
        `the claim`'s `the claimant has abandoned the excess under section 9`
    AND `the ceiling on a money or work order` `the claim` `the limits`
            EQUALS `the limits`'s `the prescribed limit`

§§ `35(3) -- correcting an order`

-- s 35(3): the tribunal "may correct an order ... WITHIN 14 DAYS after the date
-- on which the order is made, or correct a statement of the tribunal's grounds
-- ... within 14 days after the date on which the statement is issued", to
-- rectify "(a) a clerical mistake; (b) an error arising from an accidental slip
-- or omission; (c) a material miscalculation of figures, or a material mistake in
-- the description of any person, thing or matter ...; (d) a defect of form."
--
-- Fourteen days, and no power to extend it. Compare s 41(2), which allows an
-- application to set aside "within one month ... or such further period as the
-- tribunal may allow". The slip rule is the shorter period and the one that
-- cannot be stretched.
DECLARE Correction HAS
    `days since the order was made` IS A NUMBER
    `a clerical mistake` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an error arising from an accidental slip or omission` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a material miscalculation of figures or mistake in a description` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a defect of form` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the complaint is that the tribunal decided the case wrongly` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the correction` IS A Correction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mistake is one section 35(3) can rectify` IF
        `the correction`'s `a clerical mistake`
    OR  `the correction`'s `an error arising from an accidental slip or omission`
    OR  `the correction`'s `a material miscalculation of figures or mistake in a description`
    OR  `the correction`'s `a defect of form`

GIVEN `the correction` IS A Correction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tribunal may correct the order` IF
        `the mistake is one section 35(3) can rectify` `the correction`
    AND `the correction`'s `days since the order was made` AT MOST 14

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fourteen days in section 35(3) may be extended` IF
    FALSE

§§ `34 and 36 -- withdrawal, and enforcement`

-- s 34: "A claimant may AT ANY TIME withdraw the claimant's claim WHETHER OR NOT
-- a tribunal has heard the claim."
--
-- Unqualified, and it is the provision that opens the s 6(1)(b) exception. A
-- claimant who sees the tribunal heading for a s 5(5) discontinuance should
-- withdraw instead -- see scta-jurisdiction.l4.
--
-- s 36: an order under s 35(1), and certain Registrar's orders, "may be enforced
-- IN THE SAME MANNER AS A JUDGMENT OR AN ORDER MADE BY A DISTRICT COURT." So the
-- enforcement machinery is a District Court's, whatever the forum that made the
-- order.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a claimant may withdraw at any time` IF
    TRUE

GIVEN `an order was made under section 35(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order is enforceable as a District Court judgment` IF
    `an order was made under section 35(1)`

§§ `38, 40 and 41 -- appeal, finality, and setting aside`

-- s 38(1): "Any party to proceedings before a tribunal may appeal to the General
-- Division of the High Court against an order made by the tribunal under
-- section 35(1) -- (a) on any ground involving A QUESTION OF LAW; or (b) on the
-- ground that the claim was OUTSIDE THE JURISDICTION of the tribunal."
--
-- s 40: "An order of a tribunal is FINAL AND BINDING on all parties ... and,
-- EXCEPT AS PROVIDED IN SECTION 38, NO APPEAL LIES in respect of the order."
--
-- So there is no appeal on the facts. A claimant who says the tribunal believed
-- the wrong witness has no remedy under this Act.
--
-- s 41(1) is a separate route, and it is not an appeal: certain orders "may, on
-- the application of A PERSON AGGRIEVED by that order, be SET ASIDE BY THE
-- TRIBUNAL and the tribunal may make such further order as it thinks just."
-- s 41(2): the application must be made "within ONE MONTH after the date on
-- which the order was made OR SUCH FURTHER PERIOD AS THE TRIBUNAL MAY ALLOW."
--
-- The orders s 41(1) reaches are the ones made in a party's absence or on a
-- failure to appear -- s 25(3), s 29(a)(i), s 35(1) read with s 29(a)(ii) or (b),
-- and three Registrar's orders. It is the remedy for not having been there, not
-- for having lost.
--
-- Note the difference in time: one month, extendable, to set aside; fourteen days,
-- not extendable, to correct a slip. Note also that s 38 has no stated period at
-- all in this Act -- s 47(1)(h) and (i) leave the manner and procedure of appeals
-- to rules, which were not retrieved.
DECLARE Challenge HAS
    `the ground involves a question of law` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the ground is that the claim was outside the tribunal's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the ground is that the tribunal found the facts wrongly` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the order is one section 41(1) names` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the applicant is a person aggrieved by the order` IS A BOOLEAN
    `months since the order was made` IS A NUMBER
    `the tribunal has allowed a further period` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the challenge` IS A Challenge
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an appeal lies under section 38` IF
        `the challenge`'s `the ground involves a question of law`
    OR  `the challenge`'s `the ground is that the claim was outside the tribunal's jurisdiction`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an appeal lies on a question of fact` IF
    FALSE

GIVEN `the challenge` IS A Challenge
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order may be set aside under section 41` IF
        `the challenge`'s `the order is one section 41(1) names`
    AND `the challenge`'s `the applicant is a person aggrieved by the order`
    AND (    `the challenge`'s `months since the order was made` AT MOST 1
         OR  `the challenge`'s `the tribunal has allowed a further period`)

-- The two routes do different work and are not alternatives for the same
-- complaint: s 38 answers an error of law or jurisdiction, s 41 answers an order
-- made without the party there.
GIVEN `the challenge` IS A Challenge
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the party has some way of challenging the order` IF
        `an appeal lies under section 38` `the challenge`
    OR  `the order may be set aside under section 41` `the challenge`

§§ `42 -- an appeal does not stop enforcement`

-- s 42(1): "An appeal against an order made by a tribunal under section 35 ...
-- DOES NOT OPERATE AS A STAY of execution or enforcement of that order, UNLESS
-- the tribunal, a District Court or the General Division of the High Court
-- orders otherwise."
-- s 42(2): a stay is "subject to such conditions ... as the tribunal, a District
-- Court or the General Division of the High Court ... thinks fit to impose,
-- including conditions as to costs, payment into the tribunal, District Court or
-- General Division ... or the giving of security."
--
-- Three different courts can grant the stay, which is unusually generous, and
-- none of them need.
GIVEN `a stay has been ordered by the tribunal, a District Court or the General Division` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order may be enforced while an appeal is pending` IF
    NOT `a stay has been ordered by the tribunal, a District Court or the General Division`
